      "id": "train:002513:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "problem": "What ESG-related topics are assessed in the organization's supplier audits?",
      "solution": "employee rights, energy, environmental management system, health and safety, human rights, subcontracting, waste and environmental pollution",
      "attempt": "ESG (Environmental, Social, and Governance) related topics that are typically assessed during an organization's supplier audits can be quite comprehensive and cover a wide range of issues. Here’s a breakdown of what these might include:\n\n1. **Environmental Aspects:**\n   - Energy consumption and efficiency.\n   - Waste management practices.\n   - Emissions control and reduction strategies.\n   - Use of renewable energy sources.\n   - Water usage and conservation efforts.\n   - Chemical management and hazardous material handling.\n\n2. **Social Aspects:**\n   - Labor rights and working conditions.\n   - Health and safety measures for employees.\n   - Diversity and inclusion initiatives.\n   - Child labor and forced labor policies.\n   - Employee training and development programs.\n   - Community engagement and impact.\n\n3. **Governance Aspects:**\n   - Ethical business practices and anti-corruption measures.\n   - Supplier code of conduct compliance.\n   - Data privacy and security.\n   - Supply chain transparency and traceability.\n   - Risk management and crisis response plans.\n   - Corporate social responsibility (CSR) initiatives.\n\nThese audits aim to ensure that suppliers adhere to high standards across all three pillars of ESG, promoting sustainable business practices and responsible corporate citizenship. Organizations often tailor their audit criteria based on industry-specific risks and global best practices, ensuring that they align with their own sustainability goals and regulatory requirements.",
      "grading": "No"
