      "solution": "accidents with multiple casualties, critical situations, earthquakes, environmental incidents, fatalities, flooding, incidents that impact neighboring communities, natural disasters, near misses",
      "attempt": "ESG (Environmental, Social, and Governance) reporting procedures typically track a wide range of incidents that fall under these three categories. Here’s a breakdown of what each category might include:\n\n1. **Environmental Incidents:**\n   - Pollution events (air, water, soil)\n   - Waste management issues\n   - Energy consumption and carbon emissions\n   - Biodiversity impacts\n   - Natural resource depletion\n   - Environmental compliance violations\n\n2. **Social Incidents:**\n   - Labor rights violations\n   - Health and safety incidents in the workplace\n   - Community relations issues\n   - Diversity and inclusion challenges\n   - Human rights abuses\n   - Data privacy breaches\n   - Customer complaints and satisfaction levels\n\n3. **Governance Incidents:**\n   - Bribery and corruption cases\n   - Ethical misconduct by employees or executives\n   - Board composition and diversity issues\n   - Shareholder rights violations\n   - Compliance with laws and regulations\n   - Risk management failures\n\nEach organization may have its own specific criteria for what constitutes an incident within these categories, depending on their industry, size, and geographical location. The goal of tracking these incidents is to ensure transparency, accountability, and continuous improvement in sustainability practices.",
