      "id": "train:002652:qa_pairs_factoid:0015",
      "problem": "Where are Evonik's facilities or sites subject to emissions trading systems or carbon pricing mentioned in the context?",
      "solution": "Austria, Fujian, Germany, Gibbons, Maitland (Canada), Morrinsville (New Zealand), Nanping, Shanghai sites, Singapore, Ulsan (South Korea)",
      "attempt": "Evonik Industries AG, a leading specialty chemicals company based in Germany, operates in various countries around the world and is involved in several markets where emissions trading systems or carbon pricing mechanisms are implemented. Specifically, Evonik is subject to the European Union Emissions Trading System (EU ETS), which is one of the largest and most established carbon pricing schemes globally.\n\nThe EU ETS covers installations in the power and heat generation, manufacturing, and aviation sectors, including many of Evonik's production sites within the European Union. These sites are required to monitor and report their greenhouse gas emissions and surrender allowances that correspond to those emissions. This system aims to reduce greenhouse gas emissions cost-effectively by putting a price on carbon and allowing companies to trade emission allowances.\n\nWhile the EU ETS is the primary emissions trading system affecting Evonik, it's also worth noting that other regions and countries have implemented similar carbon pricing mechanisms. However, the context you're referring to specifically mentions the EU ETS as the main framework under which Evonik operates in terms of emissions trading and carbon pricing.",
