      "id": "train:005977:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "Which economic activities at Vienna Airport are classified as taxonomy-relevant under the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "solution": "Acquisition and ownership of buildings, Air transport ground handling services, Creative, arts and entertainment activities, Electricity generation using solar photovoltaic technology, Low carbon airport infrastructure",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities is a classification system that aims to provide a common language and framework for companies and financial market participants to disclose information about their environmentally sustainable economic activities. It includes six environmental objectives, such as climate change mitigation and adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nFor an activity to be considered taxonomy-relevant under the EU Taxonomy, it must:\n\n1. Substantially contribute to one or more of the six environmental objectives.\n2. Do no significant harm (DNSH) to any of the other environmental objectives.\n3. Comply with minimum social safeguards as defined in the Taxonomy Regulation.\n\nVienna Airport, like any other airport, could engage in various economic activities that might fall under the EU Taxonomy's criteria. Some potential activities include:\n\n- **Energy Efficiency:** Improvements in energy efficiency can contribute to climate change mitigation.\n- **Renewable Energy Use:** Utilizing renewable energy sources for airport operations can also contribute to climate change mitigation.\n- **Sustainable Transport Solutions:** Promoting and facilitating the use of low-emission vehicles and public transport can contribute to climate change mitigation and air quality improvement.\n- **Waste Management:** Implementing effective waste management practices can contribute to the transition to a circular economy and pollution prevention.\n- **Biodiversity Conservation:** Efforts to protect local flora and fauna and restore natural habitats can contribute to biodiversity protection.\n\nTo determine which specific activities at Vienna Airport are taxonomy-relevant, one would need to assess each activity against the detailed criteria laid out in the EU Taxonomy Regulation. This involves evaluating whether the activities meet the \"substantial contribution\" requirement, do not cause significant harm to other environmental objectives, and comply with the minimum social safeguards.\n\nFor precise and up-to-date information, it would be advisable to consult the official EU Taxonomy guidelines and any specific guidance provided by the European Commission, as well as any disclosures made by Vienna Airport regarding its sustainability initiatives and how they align with the EU Taxonomy.",
