      "question_id": "train:005951:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000016",
      "id": "train:005951:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "problem": "Which legal provisions of the German Commercial Code are cited in the sustainability report to support EU Taxonomy-aligned reporting obligations?",
      "solution": "289b HGB, 290 HGB, §264d, §§ 315b HGB",
      "attempt": "The German Commercial Code (Handelsgesetzbuch, HGB) does not directly cite specific legal provisions that mandate sustainability reporting or alignment with the EU Taxonomy. However, companies listed on stock exchanges in Germany and other EU countries are required to comply with the Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD), which came into effect in 2024, replacing the Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD). The CSRD requires more detailed and standardized sustainability disclosures.\n\nFor alignment with the EU Taxonomy, companies often refer to the Disclosure Regulation (EU) 2019/2088 (also known as the Taxonomy Regulation) and related guidelines. While these regulations do not amend the HGB directly, they influence how companies report their activities and align them with sustainable finance criteria.\n\nIn practice, companies may cite Article 19a of the Wirtschaftsrechtliche Unternehmensverpflichtungsgesetz (Wirtschaftsrechteinformationsgesetz - WIRiG), which is part of the German law and mandates certain disclosures for large companies, including sustainability information. This article indirectly supports the broader context of sustainability reporting, which can include references to the EU Taxonomy.\n\nIt's important to note that while the HGB itself does not contain specific provisions for EU Taxonomy-aligned reporting, companies must ensure their reporting practices comply with both national laws and EU directives, such as the CSRD and the Taxonomy Regulation, to meet their legal obligations regarding sustainability reporting.",
