      "problem": "Which accounting frameworks define the assets included in the total Capex under EU Taxonomy criteria?",
      "solution": "fixed assets (IAS 16), intangible assets (IAS 38), rights of use assets (IFRS 16)",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities, established under the European Union's Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR) and Taxonomy Regulation, provides detailed criteria for economic activities that can be considered environmentally sustainable. When it comes to defining which assets are included in the total Capital Expenditure (Capex) under these criteria, the framework does not directly specify a unique set of accounting standards for asset classification. However, it does provide guidelines on how certain types of expenditures can be classified as contributing to environmental objectives.\n\nFor the purposes of aligning with the EU Taxonomy, companies typically refer to International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) and, in some cases, Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP), especially if they are operating in regions where GAAP is predominant. The EU Taxonomy criteria then overlay specific sustainability requirements on top of these standard accounting practices.\n\nUnder the EU Taxonomy, Capex that qualifies must contribute to one or more of the six environmental objectives outlined in the regulation:\n\n1. Climate change mitigation\n2. Climate change adaptation\n3. Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n4. Transition to a circular economy\n5. Pollution prevention and control\n6. Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\nAssets included in Capex under the EU Taxonomy would be those that meet the technical screening criteria laid out for each of these objectives. These criteria specify what types of projects, technologies, and processes qualify as sustainable within the context of each objective. For example, investments in renewable energy infrastructure, energy efficiency measures, or pollution control technologies might be included.\n\nIt's important to note that while IFRS and GAAP provide the general framework for recognizing and measuring assets, the EU Taxonomy adds an additional layer of scrutiny focused specifically on the environmental impact and sustainability of those assets. Companies must therefore ensure that their Capex reporting not only adheres to standard accounting principles but also demonstrates alignment with the EU Taxonomy's stringent environmental criteria.",
