      "id": "test:000753:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "problem": "What sections of the Climate Delegated Act provide guidance on renewable energy sources for compliance with EU Taxonomy objectives?",
      "solution": "Annex 1, Climate Delegated Act, section 3.10, section 4.13",
      "attempt": "The Climate Delegated Act is part of the broader framework established under the EU Taxonomy Regulation (2020/852) to classify economic activities that make substantial contributions to climate change mitigation and adaptation. The act provides detailed criteria for determining whether an economic activity can be considered environmentally sustainable, including those related to renewable energy sources.\n\nHowever, it's important to note that the specific sections of the Climate Delegated Act that provide guidance on renewable energy sources for compliance with EU Taxonomy objectives are not standalone sections but rather integrated within the broader criteria set out in the regulation. Here’s how you can find relevant information:\n\n1. **Article 4 of the Delegated Act**: This article outlines the general conditions for activities to qualify as environmentally sustainable. It includes criteria for both climate change mitigation and adaptation.\n\n2. **Annex I of the Delegated Act**: This annex provides detailed technical screening criteria for various sectors, including energy. For renewable energy sources, you would look at the criteria listed under the energy sector. These criteria specify what constitutes a \"substantial contribution\" to climate change mitigation or adaptation through renewable energy activities.\n\n3. **Specific Renewable Energy Criteria**: Within Annex I, there are specific criteria for different types of renewable energy sources such as wind, solar, hydroelectric, geothermal, and biomass. Each type has its own set of requirements that must be met to ensure that the activity contributes substantially to climate change mitigation.\n\nTo get a comprehensive understanding, you should review the entire Delegated Act, focusing particularly on the sections mentioned above. Additionally, the European Commission and other regulatory bodies often provide supplementary guidance documents that can help interpret these criteria more clearly.\n\nFor the most accurate and up-to-date information, it's recommended to consult the official EU Taxonomy website or the latest versions of the Delegated Act published by the European Commission.",
