      "id": "train:006078:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "Which annexes and environmental objectives are analyzed for Taxonomy eligibility in the 2023 financial year?",
      "solution": "Annex I (Climate change mitigation), Annex I (Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources), Annex II (Climate change adaptation), Annex II (Transition to a circular economy), Annex III (Pollution prevention and control), Annex IV (Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems)",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy Regulation outlines specific criteria for economic activities that can be considered environmentally sustainable. For the 2023 financial year, the analysis of Taxonomy eligibility primarily focuses on six environmental objectives as defined by the regulation:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: Activities that contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: Activities that help adapt to the unavoidable impacts of climate change.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: Activities that promote the sustainable use and protection of water resources and marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: Activities that support the transition towards a circular economy, including waste prevention, re-use, recycling, and other forms of recovery.\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: Activities that prevent or reduce pollution.\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services**: Activities that protect and restore biodiversity and ecosystem services.\n\nRegarding annexes, the EU Taxonomy Regulation includes several annexes that provide detailed criteria for assessing whether an activity contributes substantially to one or more of these environmental objectives without causing significant harm to the others. These annexes are updated periodically to reflect new scientific evidence and technological advancements. For the 2023 financial year, companies and financial market participants would refer to the most recent versions of these annexes to determine Taxonomy eligibility.\n\nIt's important to note that compliance with the EU Taxonomy is becoming increasingly relevant not only for EU-based entities but also for international businesses operating within the EU or those looking to align their activities with sustainability goals recognized globally. Companies should consult the latest official documents from the European Commission for the most accurate and up-to-date information regarding the criteria and requirements for the 2023 financial year.",
