      "attempt": "To provide an accurate answer, I would need more specific information about the context you're referring to. The term \"taxonomy-compliant\" can vary widely depending on the specific framework or classification system being used. For example:\n\n1. **Green Taxonomy**: In the context of sustainable finance, the EU's Green Taxonomy is a classification system that aims to provide companies and investors with a common language to identify which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. These activities must contribute substantially to one or more of six environmental objectives (climate change mitigation, climate change adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems) and do no significant harm to any of the other objectives.\n\n2. **Industry Classification Systems**: In general business and financial contexts, taxonomy-compliant economic activities might refer to those that align with industry classification systems such as the North American Industry Classification System (NAICS), Standard Industrial Classification (SIC), or International Standard Industrial Classification (ISIC). These systems categorize businesses based on their primary type of economic activity.\n\n3. **Specific Regulatory Contexts**: Certain regulatory bodies may have their own definitions of taxonomy-compliant activities, especially when it comes to compliance with environmental, social, and governance (ESG) criteria.\n\nIf you could specify the particular context or framework you are interested in, I could provide more detailed information about which economic activities are considered taxonomy-compliant within that specific context.",
