      "problem": "What regulatory frameworks and sustainability standards are applied in Zalando's non-financial declaration under the EU Taxonomy?",
      "solution": "Article 8 of the EU Taxonomy Regulation, CSR Directive on Non-Financial Reporting, CSR-RUG, GRI Standards, Section 315b (1) and (3) HGB",
      "attempt": "Zalando, as a leading online platform for fashion and lifestyle products, has been increasingly focusing on sustainability and transparency in its operations. The EU Taxonomy is a classification system that aims to provide a common language and framework for companies to disclose their environmentally sustainable activities. It sets out criteria for economic activities that can be considered environmentally sustainable based on six environmental objectives.\n\nIn the context of Zalando's non-financial declaration under the EU Taxonomy, the company would need to adhere to specific regulatory frameworks and sustainability standards. These include:\n\n1. **EU Taxonomy Regulation**: This regulation provides the core criteria for what qualifies as an environmentally sustainable activity. For Zalando, this would involve assessing how its business activities contribute to one or more of the six environmental objectives outlined in the taxonomy: climate change mitigation, climate change adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\n2. **Sustainability Reporting Standards**: Zalando would also need to comply with broader sustainability reporting standards such as those provided by the Global Reporting Initiative (GRI) and the Sustainability Accounting Standards Board (SASB). These frameworks help companies report on their environmental, social, and governance (ESG) impacts comprehensively.\n\n3. **Specific Industry Standards**: In the fashion industry, there are additional standards and certifications that Zalando might consider aligning with, such as the Sustainable Apparel Coalition’s Higg Index, which measures and evaluates sustainability performance across the value chain of apparel, footwear, and home textile products.\n\n4. **National and International Regulations**: Depending on where Zalando operates, it must also comply with national regulations related to environmental protection, waste management, energy efficiency, and other sustainability-related areas.\n\n5. **Corporate Policies and Commitments**: Zalando may have internal policies and commitments that go beyond regulatory requirements, such as setting science-based targets for reducing greenhouse gas emissions, committing to sourcing materials sustainably, and ensuring fair labor practices throughout its supply chain.\n\nTo meet these requirements, Zalando would typically conduct a detailed assessment of its activities, identify those that align with the EU Taxonomy criteria, and then disclose this information in its annual sustainability report or a dedicated non-financial statement. This process involves not only meeting legal obligations but also demonstrating transparency and accountability to stakeholders regarding the company's environmental impact and sustainability efforts.",
