      "id": "train:006272:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "Which EU regulations and delegated acts were analyzed to identify economic activities eligible under the Taxonomy framework?",
      "solution": "2022/1214, 2023/2485, 2023/2486, 2023/3850, 2023/3851, Commission Delegated Regulations (EU) 2021/2139, Regulation (EU) 2020/852, Regulation (EU) 2021/2178",
      "attempt": "The Taxonomy Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2020/852), also known as the EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities, is the primary piece of legislation that outlines the criteria for determining which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. This regulation was adopted on June 18, 2020, and it forms the basis for identifying activities that contribute substantially to one or more of six environmental objectives.\n\nTo support the implementation of the Taxonomy Regulation, the European Commission has developed a series of delegated acts that provide detailed technical screening criteria for each of the environmental objectives. These delegated acts specify how an activity must perform in order to be considered environmentally sustainable according to the Taxonomy. The six environmental objectives are:\n\n1. Climate change mitigation\n2. Climate change adaptation\n3. Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n4. Transition to a circular economy\n5. Pollution prevention and control\n6. Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\nEach of these objectives has its own delegated act, which includes specific thresholds and criteria that economic activities must meet to be deemed environmentally sustainable under the Taxonomy. For example, the delegated act on climate change mitigation provides detailed criteria for assessing whether an economic activity contributes to reducing greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nIn summary, the analysis of economic activities eligible under the Taxonomy framework primarily involves the Taxonomy Regulation itself and the subsequent delegated acts that provide the technical screening criteria for each environmental objective.",
