      "problem": "What are the EU Taxonomy codes for the climate-related objectives mentioned in the context?",
      "solution": "Climate Change Adaptation: CCA, Climate Change Mitigation: CCM",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system developed by the European Union to help companies and financial market participants identify and disclose environmentally sustainable economic activities. It includes six environmental objectives, one of which is climate change mitigation and another is climate change adaptation. The taxonomy provides detailed criteria for activities that contribute substantially to each of these objectives while doing no significant harm (DNSH) to the other objectives.\n\nFor climate-related objectives specifically:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: This objective aims to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. Activities that significantly contribute to this objective must meet specific technical screening criteria outlined in the EU Taxonomy Regulation. These criteria include requirements related to greenhouse gas emissions intensity, use of renewable energy, and other factors that ensure the activity contributes effectively to reducing net greenhouse gas emissions.\n\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: This objective focuses on enhancing resilience to the actual and expected adverse impacts of climate change. Similar to climate change mitigation, there are specific criteria that activities must meet to be considered as contributing substantially to climate change adaptation without causing significant harm to the other environmental objectives.\n\nEach of these objectives has associated EU Taxonomy codes that categorize the types of activities that can be considered sustainable under the respective objective. However, the exact codes and detailed criteria are defined within the EU Taxonomy Regulation and its delegated acts, which provide a comprehensive framework for assessing whether an economic activity qualifies as environmentally sustainable.\n\nTo get the most accurate and up-to-date information regarding the specific codes and criteria for climate-related objectives, it's recommended to consult the official EU Taxonomy Regulation documents or relevant updates from the European Commission.",
