      "id": "train:006017:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "Which EU regulations and delegated acts are evaluated for their role in Taxonomy-alignment and eligibility in the context?",
      "solution": "2023/2486, Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139 Annex 1, Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139 Annex 2, Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/1214, Regulation (EU) 2020/852",
      "attempt": "In the context of evaluating EU regulations and delegated acts for their role in Taxonomy alignment and eligibility, several key pieces of legislation and related acts are considered:\n\n1. **EU Taxonomy Regulation (2020/852)**: This regulation establishes a framework to facilitate sustainable investment by providing a common language and classification system for economic activities that substantially contribute to climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as other environmental objectives. It is central to understanding which activities can be deemed environmentally sustainable.\n\n2. **Delegated Acts under the Taxonomy Regulation**: These provide detailed criteria for determining whether an economic activity qualifies as environmentally sustainable according to the taxonomy. They cover specific sectors and activities, such as energy, transport, buildings, and land use.\n\n3. **Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR) (2019/2088)**: While not directly part of the Taxonomy Regulation, SFDR complements it by requiring financial market participants to disclose information about how they integrate sustainability risks into their investment decision-making processes and about the sustainability of their financial products. This includes alignment with the EU Taxonomy.\n\n4. **Low-Carbon Benchmarks and Positive Carbon Impact Benchmark Delegated Act (EU) 2021/762**: This act sets out rules for benchmarks that can be used to measure the carbon footprint of financial products and assess their alignment with the Paris Agreement goals. It indirectly supports the Taxonomy by promoting low-carbon investments.\n\n5. **Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) (2022/1242)**: Although still pending implementation, this directive will require large companies to report on their sustainability performance using harmonized standards, including alignment with the EU Taxonomy. This will enhance transparency and comparability across companies regarding their sustainability efforts.\n\nThese regulations and delegated acts collectively form a comprehensive framework aimed at guiding sustainable finance and investment decisions within the European Union, ensuring that economic activities are aligned with the overarching goal of transitioning to a more sustainable economy.",
