      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities is primarily established through Regulation (EU) 2020/852 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 18 June 2020 on the establishment of a framework to facilitate sustainable investment, and amending Regulation (EU) 2019/2088. This regulation sets out the criteria for environmentally sustainable economic activities that contribute to climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as other environmental objectives.\n\nAdditionally, there are several related legislative references and directives that support and complement the EU Taxonomy framework:\n\n1. **Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR)** - Regulation (EU) 2019/2088: This regulation requires financial market participants and financial advisers to disclose information about the integration of sustainability risks, adverse sustainability impacts, and the promotion of environmental or social characteristics in their investment decisions and advisory processes.\n\n2. **Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD)** - Directive (EU) 2022/1242: This directive aims to improve the quality and comparability of corporate sustainability reporting by requiring large companies to report on their sustainability performance using harmonized standards.\n\n3. **Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD)** - Directive 2014/95/EU: Although this directive has been replaced by the CSRD, it previously required certain large undertakings and groups to include non-financial and diversity information in their management reports.\n\nThese legislative measures collectively aim to enhance transparency and accountability in financial markets and corporate governance regarding environmental, social, and governance (ESG) factors, aligning with the broader goals of the EU Taxonomy framework.",
