      "id": "train:006045:qa_pairs_factoid:0004",
      "problem": "Which regulatory references are cited in the context of EU Taxonomy compliance and enabling activities?",
      "solution": "Article 10 paragraph 1 letter i, EU IR 2023/2485, Taxonomy Regulation",
      "attempt": "The context of EU Taxonomy compliance and enabling activities is primarily governed by the EU Taxonomy Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2020/852). This regulation sets out the framework for determining which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable under the EU Taxonomy. Here are some key regulatory references that are often cited in this context:\n\n1. **EU Taxonomy Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2020/852)**: This is the main legislative act that establishes the criteria for determining whether an economic activity is environmentally sustainable.\n\n2. **Delegated Acts**: The European Commission has issued several delegated acts to specify the technical screening criteria for different environmental objectives under the taxonomy. These include:\n   - Delegated Act (EU) 2021/337 on climate change mitigation.\n   - Delegated Act (EU) 2021/2174 on climate change adaptation.\n   - Delegated Act (EU) 2022/1269 on sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources.\n   - Delegated Act (EU) 2022/1270 on transition to a circular economy.\n   - Delegated Act (EU) 2022/1271 on pollution prevention and control.\n\n3. **Disclosure Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2019/2088)**: Also known as the Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD), it requires large companies to disclose non-financial and diversity information, including information related to their environmental impact and how they comply with the EU Taxonomy.\n\n4. **Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD)**: This directive, which will replace the NFRD, aims to enhance corporate sustainability reporting and will require companies to report on their alignment with the EU Taxonomy.\n\nThese regulations and directives form the backbone of the EU's efforts to create a common language for sustainable finance and ensure that economic activities are aligned with the goals of the Paris Agreement and other environmental objectives. Companies and financial institutions must adhere to these guidelines when assessing and reporting on the sustainability of their activities.",
