      "problem": "What EU Taxonomy terms are highlighted in Uniper's sustainability reporting for 2023?",
      "solution": "EU Taxonomy, non-taxonomy-eligible, taxonomy alignment, taxonomy eligibility, taxonomy-aligned",
      "attempt": "To provide an accurate answer regarding which EU Taxonomy terms are highlighted in Uniper's sustainability reporting for 2023, I would need to refer directly to the latest sustainability report from Uniper. However, as of my last update, I don't have real-time access to specific documents or databases that contain this information.\n\nGenerally speaking, the EU Taxonomy is a classification system that helps companies and investors identify environmentally sustainable economic activities. It covers six environmental objectives:\n\n1. Climate change mitigation\n2. Climate change adaptation\n3. Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n4. Transition to a circular economy\n5. Pollution prevention and control\n6. Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\nUniper, being a major energy company, typically focuses on climate change mitigation and adaptation due to its core business in energy production and distribution. In their sustainability reports, they usually highlight how their projects and operations align with these objectives, especially under the EU Taxonomy criteria.\n\nFor precise details about Uniper's 2023 sustainability reporting, you should consult the official Uniper sustainability report or their website where they publish such information. This document will provide specific data and analysis related to their alignment with the EU Taxonomy.",
