      "question": "Which regulations and delegated acts define the framework for the EU Taxonomy Regulation?",
      "answer": "Annex V of the Delegated Regulation on Disclosure, Article 8 Taxonomy Regulation, Delegated Act (EU) 2021/2178 Art. 10 para. 3, Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2178, Environmental Taxonomy Regulation (EU) 2020/852",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:005968:qa_pairs_factoid:0008",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000013",
      "id": "train:005968:qa_pairs_factoid:0008",
      "problem": "Which regulations and delegated acts define the framework for the EU Taxonomy Regulation?",
      "solution": "Annex V of the Delegated Regulation on Disclosure, Article 8 Taxonomy Regulation, Delegated Act (EU) 2021/2178 Art. 10 para. 3, Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2178, Environmental Taxonomy Regulation (EU) 2020/852",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2020/852) is part of a broader set of measures aimed at facilitating sustainable investment in the European Union. The regulation itself sets out the framework for determining which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. However, it relies on several other regulatory instruments to provide more detailed guidance and criteria for assessing these activities.\n\n1. **Delegated Acts**: These are legislative acts adopted by the European Commission that specify the technical details of how the taxonomy should be applied. There are six delegated acts related to the EU Taxonomy, each focusing on one of the six environmental objectives outlined in the regulation:\n   - Climate change mitigation\n   - Climate change adaptation\n   - Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n   - Transition to a circular economy\n   - Pollution prevention and control\n   - Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\nEach delegated act provides a list of economic activities that contribute substantially to the corresponding environmental objective, as well as technical screening criteria to determine whether an activity qualifies as sustainable under the taxonomy.\n\n2. **Amendments and Updates**: Since the initial adoption of the delegated acts, there have been updates and amendments to refine and expand the scope of the taxonomy. For example, the European Commission has issued additional delegated acts to address new sectors or to clarify existing ones.\n\n3. **Complementary Regulations**: While not directly defining the taxonomy, other EU regulations and directives complement the Taxonomy Regulation by setting out broader sustainability goals and requirements. Examples include the Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD), which requires certain large companies to disclose information about their environmental, social, and governance (ESG) impacts, and the Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD), which builds on the NFRD and introduces more stringent reporting requirements aligned with the EU Taxonomy.\n\nIn summary, while the EU Taxonomy Regulation establishes the overarching framework, it is the delegated acts that provide the detailed criteria and definitions necessary for its implementation. These instruments work together to create a comprehensive system for classifying and promoting sustainable economic activities within the EU.",
      "grading": "No"
    },
