      "problem": "Which types of additions and investments are considered in the denominator for EU Taxonomy-aligned CapEx calculations?",
      "solution": "additions from business combinations (IFRS 3), additions to lease assets (IFRS 16), additions to long-term right-of-use assets (IFRS 16), gross additions to property, plant and equipment (IAS 16), total investments",
      "attempt": "The denominator for EU Taxonomy-aligned Capital Expenditure (CapEx) calculations typically includes all eligible capital expenditures that contribute to economic activities aligned with the EU Taxonomy. The EU Taxonomy is a classification system that aims to provide clarity on which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable.\n\nFor the purposes of calculating taxonomy-aligned CapEx, the denominator would generally include:\n\n1. **All Eligible CapEx**: This refers to all capital expenditures that are relevant to the economic activities of the company, including those that are not necessarily aligned with the EU Taxonomy criteria. These could be investments in property, plant, equipment, or other long-term assets.\n\n2. **Eligible Economic Activities**: Only those capital expenditures that relate to economic activities deemed environmentally sustainable according to the EU Taxonomy's technical screening criteria are included in the numerator. These activities must significantly contribute to one or more of the six environmental objectives outlined in the Taxonomy Regulation: climate change mitigation, climate change adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nIn essence, the denominator represents the total scope of capital expenditures made by a company, while the numerator focuses specifically on those expenditures that align with the EU Taxonomy's sustainability criteria. This ratio helps in assessing what proportion of a company’s investment is directed towards sustainable activities as defined by the EU Taxonomy.",
