      "problem": "What types of revenue-generating activities are included under taxonomy-eligible economic activity CCM 6.10?",
      "solution": "chartering of slots under slot charter agreements, demurrage and detention fees, net freight revenues, occasional chartering of vessels, pending voyages",
      "attempt": "Taxonomy-eligible economic activities, as defined in the EU Taxonomy Regulation, are categorized into specific codes that represent environmentally sustainable activities. Code CCM 6.10 specifically refers to \"Construction and operation of renewable energy infrastructure.\" This includes activities related to generating electricity from renewable sources.\n\nUnder CCM 6.10, revenue-generating activities typically include:\n\n1. **Generation of Electricity from Renewable Sources**: This involves the construction and operation of facilities that generate electricity from renewable energy sources such as solar, wind, hydroelectric, geothermal, and biomass.\n\n2. **Maintenance and Upkeep of Renewable Energy Infrastructure**: Activities that ensure the ongoing functionality and efficiency of renewable energy installations, including regular maintenance checks, repairs, and upgrades.\n\n3. **Operation of Renewable Energy Facilities**: The day-to-day running of renewable energy plants, which can include monitoring performance, managing operations, and ensuring compliance with environmental standards.\n\n4. **Management Services for Renewable Energy Projects**: Providing services that support the development, financing, and management of renewable energy projects, including project management, financial advisory services, and technical consultancy.\n\n5. **Sales of Renewable Energy**: Selling the electricity generated from renewable sources to end-users or through power purchase agreements (PPAs).\n\nIt's important to note that these activities must meet certain environmental and social safeguards outlined in the EU Taxonomy Regulation to be considered eligible. These safeguards ensure that the activities contribute substantially to climate change mitigation or adaptation while not causing significant harm to other environmental objectives.",
