      "attempt": "The issue you're referring to likely pertains to the International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) Foundation's Taxonomy, which is used to ensure consistent and comparable financial reporting across different entities. Economic activity 7.7, related to the acquisition and ownership of buildings, failing to achieve taxonomy alignment for the 2023 financial year suggests that there may be inconsistencies or complexities in how this particular economic activity is reported.\n\nThis failure can indicate several challenges in meeting technical screening criteria:\n\n1. **Complexity of Definitions**: The definitions and requirements for reporting on the acquisition and ownership of buildings might be complex and nuanced, leading to varied interpretations among preparers. This complexity can make it difficult to align with the standardized taxonomy.\n\n2. **Data Collection and Reporting Issues**: Gathering accurate and detailed data related to building acquisitions and ownership can be challenging. Different organizations might have varying methods of recording these transactions, leading to discrepancies when trying to fit them into a standardized format.\n\n3. **Changes in Regulations or Standards**: There could be recent changes in accounting standards or regulations that affect how buildings are reported. If these changes are not fully understood or implemented correctly, it can lead to misalignment with the taxonomy.\n\n4. **Lack of Standardization in Industry Practices**: Practices regarding the acquisition and ownership of buildings can vary widely between industries. Without clear guidelines that account for these differences, achieving alignment becomes more difficult.\n\n5. **Technical Limitations**: The technology used for financial reporting might not fully support the detailed tagging required for certain types of economic activities, such as those involving buildings. This can limit the ability to accurately map data to the taxonomy.\n\