      "problem": "Why did the right-of-use assets for company buildings fail to meet the technical screening criteria for substantial contributions to climate change mitigation under Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139?",
      "solution": "The right-of-use assets for company buildings were evaluated against the technical screening criteria for substantial contributions to climate change mitigation under Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139. However, they did not satisfy these criteria.",
      "attempt": "The failure of right-of-use assets for company buildings to meet the technical screening criteria for substantial contributions to climate change mitigation under Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139 can be attributed to several factors:\n\n1. **Nature of Right-of-Use Assets**: Right-of-use assets typically arise from lease agreements where a company has the right to use an asset (such as a building) but does not own it. The regulation likely focuses on direct ownership and control over assets that contribute to climate change mitigation, which may not be fully realized through leasing arrangements.\n\n2. **Specific Criteria in the Regulation**: Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139 sets out specific criteria for what constitutes a substantial contribution to climate change mitigation. These criteria might include requirements such as significant reductions in greenhouse gas emissions, improvements in energy efficiency, or the use of renewable energy sources. Buildings themselves, even if they are energy-efficient, might not inherently meet these criteria unless they are specifically designed or retrofitted to do so.\n\n3. **Lack of Direct Control Over Emissions**: When a company leases a building, it often does not have full control over how the building is operated or maintained. This lack of control can make it difficult to ensure that the building meets the stringent criteria set forth in the regulation for reducing emissions or promoting sustainable practices.\n\n4. **Focus on Technological and Process Innovations**: The regulation may prioritize investments in technologies and processes that directly reduce emissions or promote sustainability. Simply owning or leasing a building, even if it is used for green purposes, might not be sufficient if it does not involve innovative solutions that significantly mitigate climate change.\n\n5. **Qualitative vs. Quantitative Contributions**: The regulation might require quantitative evidence of a building's contribution to climate change mitigation, such as specific emission reduction targets or energy savings metrics. If the right-of-use asset does not provide this kind of measurable impact, it may not qualify.\n\nTo align with the regulation, companies might need to focus on acquiring or developing buildings that incorporate advanced sustainable features, actively manage their operations to meet strict environmental standards, or invest in additional measures that clearly demonstrate a substantial contribution to climate change mitigation.",
