        "paragraph": "For the financial year 2023, the Amadeus Fire Group's total Capital Expenditure (CapEx) amounted to €30,695.8 thousand. Of this total, €21,787.1 thousand, representing 70.98% of the total CapEx, was identified as Taxonomy-eligible (Category A), while €8,908.6 thousand, or 29.02%, was non-eligible (Category B). Within the Taxonomy-eligible CapEx, only a minor portion was classified as Taxonomy-aligned (Category A.1). Specifically, 'Transport by motorbikes, passenger cars and light commercial vehicles' (CCM 6.5) accounted for €16.0 thousand of CapEx, making up 0.05% of the total 2023 CapEx. This activity was confirmed to make a substantial contribution to Climate Change Mitigation, and was not eligible for Climate Change Adaptation, Water, Pollution, Circular Economy, or Biodiversity objectives. Critically, it satisfied all Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) criteria across Climate Change Mitigation, Climate Change Adaptation, Water, Pollution, Circular Economy, and Biodiversity, and also met minimum social safeguards. For this aligned portion, the proportion of Taxonomy-aligned CapEx relative to the 2022 total CapEx was 0.02%.\n\nThe majority of Taxonomy-eligible CapEx, amounting to €21,771.2 thousand (70.93% of total 2023 CapEx), fell under Category A.2, being Taxonomy-eligible but not environmentally sustainable (not Taxonomy-aligned). This category includes several activities, all deemed eligible for Climate Change Mitigation ('EL') but not eligible ('N/EL') for the other five environmental objectives (Climate Change Adaptation, Water, Pollution, Circular Economy, and Biodiversity), and for which DNSH criteria and minimum safeguards were not confirmed or satisfied, hence they are not aligned.\n\nKey activities in Category A.2 are:\n\n1.  **Operation of personal mobility devices, cycle logistics** (CCM 6.4): With CapEx of €41.2 thousand (0.13% of total 2023 CapEx), this activity was eligible for Climate Change Mitigation. However, it was not confirmed to satisfy DNSH criteria or minimum social safeguards, as indicated by the blank entries for these columns in the table, stemming from an inability to sufficiently determine the implementation of minimum social safeguards in 2023. Its eligible CapEx proportion from 2022 was 0.23%.\n\n2.  **Transport by motorbikes, passenger cars and light commercial vehicles** (CCM 6.5): This larger portion of the activity, with CapEx of €1,889.7 thousand (6.16% of total 2023 CapEx), was eligible for Climate Change Mitigation. Similar to CCM 6.4, the DNSH criteria and minimum safeguards were not confirmed, resulting in its non-alignment. Its eligible CapEx proportion from 2022 was 6.02%.\n\n3.  **Installation, maintenance and repair of charging stations for electric vehicles in buildings (and parking spaces attached to buildings)** (CCM 7.4): With CapEx of €32.4 thousand (0.11% of total 2023 CapEx), this activity was eligible for Climate Change Mitigation. An alignment check was not carried out for this expenditure due to its immateriality and absence of data or evidence, leading to its aligned proportion being 0.00% for 2022.\n\n4.  **Acquisition and ownership of buildings** (CCM 7.7): This constituted the largest portion of eligible CapEx at €19,186.6 thousand (62.51% of total 2023 CapEx). While eligible for Climate Change Mitigation, the right-of-use assets for company buildings did not satisfy the technical screening criteria for a substantial contribution, thus DNSH and minimum safeguards were not confirmed. Its eligible CapEx proportion from 2022 was 75.47%.\n\n5.  **Data processing, hosting and related activities** (CCM 8.1): With CapEx of €621.2 thousand (2.02% of total 2023 CapEx), this activity was eligible for Climate Change Mitigation. Similar to CCM 7.4, an alignment check was not conducted due to immaterial expenditure and lack of data, resulting in its aligned proportion being 0.00% for 2022.\n\nOverall, the total Taxonomy-eligible CapEx (A.1+A.2) for 2023 was €21