      "solution": "Austria, Brazil, CAN, UAE, UK, US",
      "attempt": "Several regions around the world have implemented green tariffs or onsite renewable energy generation to support their sustainability goals. Here are some notable examples:\n\n1. **Europe**: Many European countries have been at the forefront of adopting green tariffs and renewable energy policies. For instance:\n   - Germany has a well-established system of feed-in tariffs that supports renewable energy production.\n   - Denmark is known for its high penetration of wind power and has policies that encourage both large-scale and small-scale renewable energy projects.\n\n2. **North America**:\n   - In the United States, several states like California, New York, and Texas have implemented green pricing programs where customers can choose to pay a premium for electricity generated from renewable sources.\n   - Canada also has various green tariff options, particularly in provinces like Ontario and British Columbia, which promote renewable energy through different incentive programs.\n\n3. **Asia**:\n   - Japan has introduced feed-in tariffs (FIT) to promote renewable energy, especially solar and wind power.\n   - China, being the world's largest producer of renewable energy, has implemented various policies including green tariffs to support the growth of renewable energy within its grid.\n\n4. **South America**:\n   - Brazil has a robust bioenergy sector and has implemented policies to integrate renewable energy into its national grid, including green tariffs for certain types of renewable energy.\n\n5. **Australia**:\n   - Australia has seen significant uptake in rooftop solar installations, partly due to government incentives and green tariff schemes offered by some utilities.\n\nThese regions and countries are actively working towards reducing carbon emissions and increasing the share of renewable energy in their energy mix, often through innovative tariff structures and direct investment in renewable energy technologies.",
