      "question_id": "train:001096:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000014",
      "id": "train:001096:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "What sustainability-focused measures were implemented in Poland's new-build projects to enhance biodiversity and green infrastructure?",
      "solution": "25,000 sqm of green roofs, 6,500 sqm of rain gardens, 86,000 sqm of green spaces, more than 800 trees, rainwater irrigation system, shrubs and flower meadows",
      "attempt": "Poland, like many other countries, has been increasingly focusing on integrating sustainability into its new-build projects to enhance biodiversity and improve green infrastructure. While specific measures can vary widely depending on the project and location within Poland, here are some common initiatives that have been implemented:\n\n1. **Green Roofs and Walls**: Many new buildings incorporate green roofs and walls which not only help in reducing urban heat islands but also provide habitats for local flora and fauna, enhancing biodiversity.\n\n2. **Rainwater Harvesting Systems**: These systems collect rainwater from rooftops and store it for later use, reducing the strain on municipal water supplies and helping to manage stormwater runoff more effectively.\n\n3. **Native Plant Species**: Using native plant species in landscaping helps to support local wildlife and ecosystems, as these plants are adapted to the local climate and soil conditions.\n\n4. **Biodiversity Corridors**: In larger developments, corridors of green space are often designed to connect fragmented natural areas, allowing wildlife to move between habitats and improving overall biodiversity.\n\n5. **Sustainable Drainage Systems (SuDS)**: These systems mimic natural drainage processes to manage surface water runoff, reduce pollution, and enhance groundwater recharge.\n\n6. **Energy Efficiency Measures**: Implementing energy-efficient designs and technologies reduces the environmental impact of buildings, contributing to overall sustainability goals.\n\n7. **Waste Management Plans**: Effective waste management plans are integrated into construction and operation phases to minimize waste and promote recycling.\n\n8. **Urban Forestry Programs**: Increasing tree cover in urban areas through planting programs can significantly enhance air quality, reduce noise pollution, and provide habitat for birds and insects.\n\n9. **Community Gardens and Parks**: Incorporating community gardens and parks into new developments provides green spaces for residents while supporting local ecosystems.\n\n10. **Sustainable Materials**: Using sustainable building materials that are locally sourced or recycled can reduce the carbon footprint of construction projects.\n\nThese measures reflect a broader trend towards sustainable urban development in Poland, aimed at creating more livable cities that are resilient to environmental challenges and supportive of biodiversity. However, the specifics of how these measures are implemented can vary based on local regulations, available resources, and the particular needs of each project.",
      "grading": "No"
    },
